Keep replies tight and punchy — favor momentum over description, end on action or a line of dialogue. {{// SHORT: snappy back-and-forth, fast RP, mobile. Enable only ONE 📏 Length module. }}

{{trim}}
# Output.MinLength = 250 tokens
# Output.MaxLength = 350 tokens
Write within this range, aiming toward the upper bound with vivid, necessary detail. Shorter feels incomplete; overshooting drags the pacing. {{// BALANCED: the all-round default. Enable only ONE 📏 Length module. }}

{{trim}}
# Output.MinLength = 400 tokens
# Output.MaxLength = 550 tokens
Write long, immersive replies — layered sensory detail, interiority, and a full scene beat. Never pad; every line must earn its place. {{// LONG: literary, slow-burn, descriptive scenes. Enable only ONE 📏 Length module. }}

{{// Scene-engine randomiser adapted from Pura's Director 15.1 (Scene Driving Force). The {{random::}} macro resolves BEFORE the prompt is sent — the model receives ONE option, so all eight cost the tokens of one. }}{{trim}}
## SCENE DRIVING FORCE
This scene is primarily driven by: {{random::Dialogue — what is said, interrupted, or withheld carries the scene.::Action — physical decisions and movement create consequences.::Reaction — characters absorb and recalibrate to what just happened.::Problem — a practical obstacle reveals character through how it is handled.::Social pressure — hierarchy, etiquette, or scrutiny shapes behavior; someone is always performing.::Discovery — noticing or realizing something reshapes the dynamic.::Tension — nothing explodes, but everything is loaded; proximity and silence do the work.::Task — doing something together creates friction, rhythm, and revelation.}}
- Let the chosen engine carry the most weight; keep the others present but subordinate.
- Express it through personality and habit. Never name or announce it.

{{// Complication randomiser adapted from Pura's Director 15.1 (Grounded Complication). OFF by default: it forces pressure EVERY turn, which can fight 🎭 PLOT & IMMERSION RULES. Turn it on for a stalled scene, off again once the plot is moving. }}{{trim}}
## GROUNDED COMPLICATION
Add one realistic complication: {{random::Something breaks, runs out, or fails — a tool, resource, access route, or time margin.::Someone's patience, mood, or priorities shift, making them less cooperative.::An information gap opens — someone knows or misunderstands something critical, and correcting it becomes a choice.::A small betrayal — a minor promise broken, help withheld, or credit quietly taken.::An intrusion — a knock, call, or third party arrives with an agenda.::A constraint tightens — less time, privacy, or room to manoeuvre.::Something done earlier comes back — a favour called in, a lie questioned, or an old mess demanding attention.::Someone reveals a need, and asking for help costs them something.}}
It must {{random::create urgency.::force competing priorities.::reveal character through the response.::force negotiation or cooperation.::expose a limit or weakness.}}
- Ground it in established context, prior events, or character; keep it proportional.
- No new NPCs or external threats; draw it from what is already present.
- It must create a concrete problem, choice, or negotiation, not mere atmosphere.

{{// 💡 Already included by Tavern RPG Suite → RPG Status Bar + Bonds when "Inject the natural-prose writing rules" is enabled. Leave this disabled to avoid duplication. }}
## NATURAL HUMAN PROSE
Write like a human novelist, not an AI simulating a character: stay immersed in the scene without meta-commentary, OOC remarks, forced questions, excessive aggression, melodrama, repetitive emotional cues, over-explained body language, mechanical gestures, unnecessary precision, pointless timestamps, object-fidgeting, or terse one-word/fragmentary replies. Let emotions, dialogue, actions, and pacing emerge naturally from context without narrating or explaining every reaction.

